<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Milan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Milan is a major city of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>. In the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> it served as an imperial residence, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-milan">Edict of Milan</a> of 313 granted toleration to Christians. Carpets from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a>'s workshops are now held in collections in cities including Milan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/milan.jpg" alt="Milan" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jiuguang Wang · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Milan_Cathedral_from_Piazza_del_Duomo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Milan lies in the north-western part of the Po Valley, roughly halfway between the river Po to the south and the foothills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, with the lakes Como, Maggiore and Lugano to the north, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ticino">Ticino</a> to the west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adda">Adda</a> to the east. The land is flat, and the highest point is 122 m above sea level. The Celtic name Medhelanon, later Latinized as Mediolanum, is usually explained as a settlement in the midst of the plain, or between watercourses, given the Olona, Lambro and Seveso rivers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to the account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, the Gaulish king Ambicatus sent his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bellovesus">Bellovesus</a> into northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and Bellovesus is said to have founded the settlement in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-priscus">Tarquinius Priscus</a>. Around 590 BCE a Celtic tribe of the Insubres, belonging to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golasecca-culture">Golasecca culture</a>, settled the site as Medhelanon, around a sanctuary in the shape of an ellipse with axes of 443 m and 323 m, near the present Piazza della Scala. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> conquered the city in 222 BCE. From 284 to 402 CE it was capital of the western part of the Roman Empire.</p><p>In the late fourth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Saint Ambrose</a>, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Milan, reshaped the centre and built the great basilicas at the city gates: Sant'Ambrogio, San Nazaro in Brolo, San Simpliciano and Sant'Eustorgio, which still stand. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-middle-ages">Late Middle Ages</a> the wealthy Duchy of Milan was one of the great forces behind the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, and the Visconti family, major patrons of the arts, made the city a centre of Gothic art. Work on the cathedral began in 1386 and ended in 1877. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a> worked in Milan from 1482 until 1499 and painted the Virgin of the Rocks and The Last Supper there.</p><p>In the early modern period Milan was a major centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-enlightenment">Italian Enlightenment</a>, and its cultural and political struggle against Austrian domination was crucial in the reunification of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a>. From the 19th century the city led the industrial and financial development of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> its population grew with migration, first from poorer and rural parts of Italy and later from abroad, and it peaked at 1,743,427 in 1973.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Milan is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> and the second-largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, with a population of 1,362,863. It is regarded as the country's industrial and financial centre and is a world centre of fashion. The Cathedral, the largest church in Italy, the Pinacoteca di Brera and the Castello Sforzesco, which holds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a>'s last sculpture, the Rondanini Pietà, are among its principal monuments. Of the Roman city, few remains survive, notably the Colonne di San Lorenzo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Milan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 260 a great raid reached Italy before Gallienus defeated it near <b>Milan</b>, and in 268 Claudius II defeated another army near Lake Garda.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alemanni">Alemanni · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Duchy of <b>Milan</b> grew out of the signoria, or lordship, that the Visconti family had built over Milan and the cities of Lombardy since the late thirteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Parma and Piacenza, two prosperous cities of the Po plain on the Via Emilia, had belonged to the Duchy of <b>Milan</b> for much of the late Middle Ages before Pope Julius II annexed them to the Papal States in 1512.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Netherlands, the Dutch Revolt that broke out in 1566–1568 tied down the Army of Flanders, supplied overland by the Spanish Road from <b>Milan</b>, for eighty years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Industrialisation from the 1890s concentrated in the triangle of <b>Milan</b>, Turin and Genoa, where textiles, steel, engineering and firms such as Fiat, founded in 1899, and Pirelli flourished, helped by hydroelectric power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Italian Wars the Confederates briefly controlled the Duchy of <b>Milan</b> and won the Battle of Novara in 1513, but their defeat by Francis I of France at the Battle of Marignano in 1515 ended their ambitions as a great power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Military</a></li></ul></section>
